  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe aforementioned strong ridge to slide east into the Ohio \nRiver Valley Wednesday, allowing a fast moving shortwave and \nassociated surface low to lift northeast from the Great Plains \ninto the Upper Midwest. This will drag a cool front through the\nCWA on Thursday. Ahead of the front, strong return flow evident\nby 850mb winds increasing around 50kts, to allow for breezy \nsouth winds to advect in warmer temps. Well above normal \ntemperatures in the upper 50s/lower 60s Tuesday through Saturday\nare forecast! With the NBM dewpoints verifying too high as of \nlate, we may need to watch for any fire wx concerns given our \ndry antecedent conditions and gusty southwest winds \nWednesday/Thursday. \n\nRegarding precipitation chances, ensemble forecast soundings and \nlayer RH progs suggest there will be plenty of dry air to overcome \nresulting in mainly sprinkles/virga Wednesday night aside from what \nthe NBM shows (10-20% PoPs and zero QPF). These PoPs may end up\nlowering further in later forecasts. The western CONUS upper \nlow to then eject a shortwave into the Plains, resulting in \n15-60% PoPs Friday afternoon through Saturday. Taking a look at \na dprog/dt of the NBM QPF, there is a downward trend in >0.1\" \nprobabilities ending 12z Sat with highest values (40-50%) \nnorthwest of Cedar Rapids. There remains considerable \ndifferences with the evolution of this wave in the deterministic\nand ensemble runs of the GFS/ECMWF. Something to watch will be \nwhether there will be any phasing of a northern stream wave \nSaturday as the 00z ECMWF suggests, which could bring a slower \nsystem and higher PoPs along a cold front.",
